{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2229 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1777a36dc8216feb88f482b72802816f50ab4e02dbbcae8201562439d30488bc" :edb/db-before-t 2228 :edb/db-after-t 2229 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535541 50 "2026-10-14T10:14:21.138Z" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051350 1075 17592186051351 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186051350 1075 17592186051352 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186051350 1075 17592186513786 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051350 1075 17592186514107 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051350 1078 17592186513786 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051362 1075 17592186051363 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186051362 1075 17592186051364 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186051362 1075 17592186051365 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186051362 1075 17592186051366 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186051362 1075 17592186051367 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186051362 1075 17592186513687 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051362 1075 17592186513775 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051362 1075 17592186514022 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051362 1075 17592186514114 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051362 1075 17592186514142 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186051362 1078 17592186513775 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186053961 1075 17592186053962 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186053961 1075 17592186053963 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186053961 1075 17592186053964 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186053961 1075 17592186053965 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186053961 1075 17592186053966 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186053961 1075 17592186513552 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186053961 1075 17592186513637 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186053961 1075 17592186513748 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186053961 1075 17592186514067 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186053961 1075 17592186514099 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186053961 1078 17592186513637 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054425 1071 "List the elements of the following set:\n$A = \\big\\{a, c,3, \\{c,3 \\}, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}\\big\\}$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054425 1071 "List the elements of the following set: \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{a, c,3, \\{c,3 \\}, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}\\big\\}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054425 1074 "The elements of the set $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\na, \\quad c, \\quad 3, \\quad \\{c,3\\}, \\quad \\emptyset, \\quad \\{\\emptyset\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\n- The elements $c,3$, and $\\{c,3\\}$ are three separate entities. The first two are the elements $c$ and $3$, and the third is a set *containing* these two elements.\n- The elements $\\emptyset$ and $\\{\\emptyset \\}$ are also separate entities. The first one is the empty set (i.e., the set containing no elements), while the second is a set that contains the empty set." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054425 1074 "The elements of the set $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\na, \\quad c, \\quad 3, \\quad \\{c,3\\}, \\quad \\emptyset, \\quad \\{\\emptyset\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\n- The elements $c,3,$ and $\\{c,3\\}$ are three separate entities. The first two are the elements $c$ and $3,$ and the third is a set *containing* these two elements.\n\n- The elements $\\emptyset$ and $\\{ \\emptyset \\}$ are also separate entities. The first one is the empty set (i.e., the set containing no elements), while the second is a set that contains the empty set." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054426 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a set?\n\n1. $\\{2, 4, 6 \\}$\n2. $\\{\\{2 \\}, \\{4, 6 \\} \\}$\n3. $2, 4, 6$\n4. $\\emptyset$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054426 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a set?\n\nI. $\\{ 2, 4, 6 \\}$\n\nII. $\\{ \\{ 2 \\}, \\{ 4, 6 \\} \\}$\n\nIII. $2, 4, 6$\n\nIV. $\\emptyset$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054426 1074 "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $\\emptyset$.\n\n- $\\{2, 4, 6 \\}$ is a set. The elements are $2$, $4$, and $6$, and they are listed between two braces.\n- $\\{\\{2 \\}, \\{4, 6 \\} \\}$ is a set. The elements are $\\{2 \\}$ and $\\{4, 6 \\}$, and are listed between the two outermost braces.\n- $2, 4, 6$ is *not* a set. The numbers $2$, $4$, and $6$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n- $\\emptyset$ is the empty set. Therefore, it is a set.\n\nIn conclusion, only III is not a set." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054426 1074 "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $\\emptyset.$\n\n- $\\{ 2, 4, 6 \\}$ is a set. The elements are $2,$ $4,$ and $6,$ and they are listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 2 \\}, \\{ 4, 6 \\} \\}$ is a set. The elements are $\\{ 2 \\}$ and $\\{ 4, 6 \\},$ and are listed between the two outermost braces.\n\n- $2, 4, 6$ is *not* a set. The numbers $2,$ $4,$ and $6$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\emptyset$ is the empty set. Therefore, it is a set.\n\nIn conclusion, only III is not a set." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054427 1071 "Write the following statement using element notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ and $2$ are elements of a set $S$, but $3$, $4$, and $5$ are not." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054427 1071 "Write the following statement using element notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ and $2$ are elements of a set $S,$ but $3,$ $4,$ and $5$ are not." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054427 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S$, we write $a \\in S$. Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S$, we write $a \\notin S$.\n\n- Since $1$ and $2$ are elements of $S$, we write $1,2 \\in S$.\n- Since $3$, $4$, and $5$ are *not* elements of $S$, we write $3,4,5 \\notin S$.\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$$\n1,2 \\in S\\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad 3,4,5 \\notin S\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054427 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a \\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a \\notin S.$\n\n- Since $1$ and $2$ are elements of $S,$ we write $1,2 \\in S.$\n\n- Since $3,$ $4,$ and $5$ are *not* elements of $S,$ we write $3,4,5 \\notin S.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$$\n1,2 \\in S\\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad 3,4,5 \\notin S\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054428 1071 "Express the following sets using an ellipsis:\n\n- The set $X$ of all positive integers greater than or equal to $10$\n\n- The set $Y$ of integers less than zero\n\n- The set $A$ of all natural numbers greater than $5$ and less than $5\\, 005$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054428 1071 "Express the following sets using an ellipsis:\n\n- The set $X$ of all positive integers greater than or equal to $10$\n- The set $Y$ of integers less than zero\n- The set $A$ of all natural numbers greater than $5$ and less than $5\\, 005$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054428 1074 "Remember that an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element, or \"and so on\" if not.\n\n- To express the set $X$, we start with the first positive integer greater than or equal to $10$, which is $10$. The next integer is $11$, the one after that is $12$, and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n$X = \\{10, 11, 12, \\ldots \\}$\n- To express the set $Y$, we start with the first integer less than zero, which is $-1$. The next integer less than zero is $-2$, the one after that is $-3$, and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n$Y = \\{-1, -2, -3, \\ldots \\}$\nHowever, note that it is conventional to write a set of numbers from least to greatest, just like is shown on the number line. Writing the elements of $Y$ from least to greatest, we have\n$Y = \\{\\ldots, -3, -2, -1\\}$.\n- To express the set $A$, we start with the first positive integer greater than $5$, which is $6$. The next integer is $7$, the one after that is $8$, and so on up to $5\\, 004$ (the largest integer less than $5\\, 005$). So, we have\n$A = \\{6, 7, 8, \\ldots, 5004\\}$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054428 1074 "Remember that an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element, or \"and so on\" if not.\n\n- To express the set $X,$ we start with the first positive integer greater than or equal to $10,$ which is $10.$ The next integer is $11,$ the one after that is $12,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\nX = \\{10, 11, 12, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\n- To express the set $Y,$ we start with the first integer less than zero, which is $-1.$ The next integer less than zero is $-2,$ the one after that is $-3,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\nY = \\{ -1, -2, -3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that it is conventional to write a set of numbers from least to greatest, just like is shown on the number line. Writing the elements of $Y$ from least to greatest, we have\n\n$$\nY = \\{ \\ldots , -3, -2, -1\\}.\n$$\n\n- To express the set $A,$ we start with the first positive integer greater than $5,$ which is $6.$ The next integer is $7,$ the one after that is $8,$ and so on up to $5\\, 004$ (the largest integer less than $5\\, 005$). So, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\{6, 7, 8, \\ldots , 5004\\}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054512 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right]$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054512 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right].$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054512 1074 "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right] &= \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)^3\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)^9 \\\\[3pt] &= \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)\\right)^3\\cdot \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)\\right)^9 \\\\[3pt] &= (2\\cdot 3 -1)^3\\cdot (3-2)^9 \\\\[3pt] &= 5^3 \\cdot 1^9 \\\\[3pt] & = 125. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054512 1074 "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right]   &= \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)^3\\cdot  \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)\\right)^3\\cdot  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)\\right)^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= (2\\cdot 3 -1)^3\\cdot (3-2)^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= 5^3 \\cdot 1^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    & = 125.\n}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054513 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. Determine $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3$.\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054513 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Determine $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3.$\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054513 1074 "From the graph, we find\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)((2 - 7x)/(xf(x) + 2))^{3} &= (lim_(x → 2)(2 - 7x)/(xf(x) + 2))^{3} \\\\\n&= ((2 - 7(2))/((2 \\cdot 3) + 2))^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-12}{8})^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-3}{2})^{3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{27}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054513 1074 "From the graph, we find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3.$ Therefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{(\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2-7(2)}{(2\\cdot 3)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-12}{8})}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-3}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054518 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054518 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054518 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}\n=\\dfrac{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\sqrt{x+8}}{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt{x+8} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(x+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(x-2) & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2} & =\\frac{3}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054518 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2} =\\dfrac{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\sqrt{x+8}}{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}(x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)\\sqrt{x + 8} &= \\sqrt{lim_(x → 1)(x + 8)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 8} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)(x - 2) &= 1 - 2 \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)\\frac{\\sqrt{x + 8}}{x - 2} &= (3)/((-1)) \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054519 1074 "According to the power rule and the quotient rules,\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}{(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1})}^{2/3} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)})}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that the limit of the numerator is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2} f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n We also calculate the limit of the denominator as\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1) & =(-2)^{2}-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}{(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1})}^{2/3} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)})}^{2/3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054519 1074 "According to the power rule and the quotient rules,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 2)((f(x))/(x^{2} - 1))^{2/3} &= ((lim_(x → - 2)f(x))/(lim_(x → - 2)(x^{2} - 1)))^{2/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that the limit of the numerator is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2} f(x)=1\n$$\n\nWe also calculate the limit of the denominator as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 2)(x^{2} - 1) &= (-2)^{2} - 1 = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 2)((f(x))/(x^{2} - 1))^{2/3} &= ((lim_(x → - 2)f(x))/(lim_(x → - 2)(x^{2} - 1)))^{2/3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3})^{2/3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{9}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054529 1071 "Evaluate $(4 + 3\\,\\textrm{i}) + (1 + 5\\,\\textrm{i})$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054529 1071 "Evaluate $(4 + 3\\,\\textrm{i}) + (1 + 5\\,\\textrm{i}).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054529 1074 "To add complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4 + 3\\textrm{i}) + (1 + 5\\textrm{i}) &= (4 + 3\\textrm{i}) + (1 + 5\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= (4 + 1) + (3 + 5)\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 5 + 8\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054529 1074 "To add complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+3\\,\\text{i})+(1+5\\,\\text{i}) & =(4+3\\,\\text{i})+(1+5\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(4+1)+(3+5)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =5+8\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054530 1071 "Find the value of $(5 + 4\\,\\textrm{i}) - (1 + 2\\,\\textrm{i})$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054530 1071 "Find the value of $(5 + 4\\,\\textrm{i}) - (1 + 2\\,\\textrm{i}).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054530 1074 "To subtract complex numbers, we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5 + 4\\textrm{i}) - (1 + 2\\textrm{i}) &= (5 + 4\\textrm{i}) - (1 + 2\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= (5 - 1) + (4 - 2)\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 4 + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054530 1074 "To subtract complex numbers, we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+4\\,\\text{i})-(1+2\\,\\text{i}) & =(5+4\\,\\text{i})-(1+2\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(5-1)+(4-2)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =4+2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054531 1071 "Evaluate $\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{-9} \\right) + (3 - \\sqrt{-16}).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054531 1071 "Evaluate $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{-9} \\right) + (3 - \\sqrt{-16})$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054531 1074 "First, we simplify the two complex numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{-9}) + (3 - \\sqrt{-16}) &= (\\frac{1}{2} + 3\\textrm{i}) + (3 - 4\\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo add these two complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2} + 3\\textrm{i}) + (3 - 4\\textrm{i}) &= (\\frac{1}{2} + 3) + (3 + (-4))\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2} + 3) + (3 - 4)\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2} - 1\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2} - \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054531 1074 "First, we simplify the two complex numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{-9})+(3-\\sqrt{-16}) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+3\\,\\text{i})+(3-4\\,\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo add these two complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}+3\\,\\text{i})+(3-4\\,\\text{i}) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+3)+(3+(-4))\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}+3)+(3-4)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-1\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054532 1071 "Find the value of $5\\,\\textrm{i} - 8\\,\\textrm{i}$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054532 1071 "Find the value of $5\\,\\textrm{i} - 8\\,\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054532 1074 "To subtract two complex numbers, we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately.\n\nIn this case, $5\\,\\textrm{i}$ and $8\\,\\textrm{i}$ are purely imaginary numbers. Therefore, we only need to subtract their imaginary parts, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\textrm{i} - 8\\textrm{i} = (5 - 8)\\textrm{i} &= -3\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054532 1074 "To subtract two complex numbers, we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately.\n\nIn this case, $5\\,\\textrm{i}$ and $8\\,\\textrm{i}$ are purely imaginary numbers. Therefore, we only need to subtract their imaginary parts, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,\\text{i}-8\\,\\text{i}=(5-8)\\,\\text{i} & =-3\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054604 1038 17592186514370 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054605 1038 17592186514399 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054618 1038 17592186514395 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054618 1038 17592186514427 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054619 1038 17592186514450 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054619 1038 17592186514473 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054620 1038 17592186514394 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054620 1038 17592186514413 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054620 1038 17592186514457 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054621 1038 17592186514379 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054702 1038 17592186514416 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054848 1038 17592186514460 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054849 1038 17592186514414 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054849 1038 17592186514475 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054850 1038 17592186514419 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054850 1038 17592186514420 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054850 1038 17592186514474 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054851 1038 17592186514367 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054851 1038 17592186514425 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054893 1050 420.0 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054899 1050 900.0 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054904 1050 420.0 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054970 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054970 1071 "Which of the following is an element of the set $C = {{a}, b, {c}, d, {e}}$?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054970 1071 "Which of the following is an element of the set $C=\\{\\{a\\},\\,b,\\,\\{c\\},\\,d,\\,\\{e\\}\\}?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054970 1074 "The elements of our set are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{a\\},\\,b,\\,\\{c\\},\\,d,\\,\\{e\\}\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, the only element that belongs to the set is $\\{e\\}.$\n\n**Watch out!** Note that $e$ is *not* an element of our set. Our set contains an element $\\{e\\}$ that is a set itself." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054972 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054972 1071 "If $a, b$, and $c$ are elements of $S$, but $d$ and $e$ are not, then which of the following is true?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186054972 1071 "If $a,b,$ and $c$ are elements of $S$, but $d$ and $e$ are not, then which of the following is true?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186054972 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $a,b,$ and $c$ are elements of $S,$ we write $a,b,c\\in S.$\n\n- Since $d$ and $e$ are *not* elements of $S,$ we write $d,e\\notin S.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $a,b,c\\in S$ and $d,e\\notin S$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186055454 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186055454 1071 "$-5\\,\\text{i}+\\text{i}=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186055454 1071 "What is $-5\\textrm{i} + \\textrm{i}$?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186055454 1074 "To add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nIn this case, $-5\\,\\text{i}$ and $\\text{i}$ are purely imaginary numbers. Therefore, we only need to add their imaginary parts, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\,\\text{i}+\\text{i} & =-5\\,\\text{i}+1\\,\\text{i}=(-5+1)\\,\\text{i}=-4\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186055954 1064 "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nlim_(x → a)(f(x))^{n} = (lim_(x → a)f(x))^{n} = L^{n}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}$ by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(3x - 4)^{8} &= (lim_(x → 2)(3x - 4))^{8} \\\\\n&= (3 \\cdot 2 - 4)^{8} \\\\\n&= 2^{8} \\\\\n&= 256\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186055954 1064 "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={({lim}_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186055955 1064 "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nlim_(x → a)\\sqrt[n]{f(x)} = \\sqrt[n]{lim_(x → a)f(x)} = \\sqrt[n]{L}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}$ by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)\\sqrt{3x - 2} &= \\sqrt{lim_(x → 2)(3x - 2)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3 \\cdot 2 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186055955 1064 "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{{lim}_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186055956 1064 "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nthen for any fraction $p/q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like $\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}$ by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(y → 64)y^{-1/6} &= (lim_(y → 64)y)^{-1/6} \\\\\n&= (64)^{-1/6} \\\\\n&= (2^{6})^{-1/6} \\\\\n&= 2^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186055956 1064 "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={({lim}_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186055967 1064 "To add complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 + 5\\textrm{i}) + (2 - 3\\textrm{i}) &= (3 + 5\\textrm{i}) + (2 - 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= (3 + 2) + (5 + (-3))\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= (3 + 2) + (5 - 3)\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 5 + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we write\n\n$$\n(a + b\\textrm{i}) + (c + d\\textrm{i}) = (a + c) + (b + d)\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nSubtracting complex numbers works in the same way: we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 + 5\\textrm{i}) - (2 - 3\\textrm{i}) &= (3 + 5\\textrm{i}) - (2 - 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= (3 - 2) + (5 - (-3))\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= (3 - 2) + (5 + 3)\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 1 + 8\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we write\n\n$$\n(a + b\\textrm{i}) - (c + d\\textrm{i}) = (a - c) + (b - d)\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186055967 1064 "To add complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+5\\text{i})+(2-3\\text{i}) & =(3+5\\text{i})+(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(3+2)+(5+(-\\,3))\\text{i} \\\\  & =(3+2)+(5-3)\\text{i} \\\\  & =5+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we write\n\n$$\n(a + b\\textrm{i}) + (c + d\\textrm{i}) = (a + c) + (b + d)\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nSubtracting complex numbers works in the same way: we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+5\\text{i})-(2-3\\text{i}) & =(3+5\\text{i})-(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(3-2)+(5-(-\\,3))\\text{i} \\\\  & =(3-2)+(5+3)\\text{i} \\\\  & =1+8\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we write\n\n$$\n(a + b\\textrm{i}) - (c + d\\textrm{i}) = (a - c) + (b - d)\\textrm{i}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186056036 1064 "A **set** is a collection of objects known as **elements** or **members**.\n\nUsually, the elements of a set have some property in common. For example, a set could be a class at school where the students are the members of the set. The common property is that all the students are members of the class.\n\nTo write a set, we list the elements between two braces (curly brackets). Commas are used to separate the elements.\n\nSo, for example, the set of the primary colors would be written as\n\n$$\nS =\\{ \\text{yellow}, \\: \\text{red}, \\: \\text{blue} \\}.\n$$\n\nThe elements of this set are $\\text{yellow},$ $\\text{red},$ and $\\text{blue}.$\n\nA set that does not contain any elements is known as the **null set** or **empty set** and is written as either\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\quad  \\text{or} \\quad \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- *A set may contain other sets as elements.* For instance, the set\n\n$$\nT = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 4, \\: \\{5, 6\\}\\}\n$$\n\ncontains the set $\\{5, 6\\}$ as an element. However, it does *not* contain the elements $5$ and $6$.\n\n- *Order doesn't matter.* The order in which the elements are listed is irrelevant. For instance, the following sets represent the same thing:\n\n$$\nA = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3 \\} = \\{2, \\: 3, \\: 1\\}\n$$\n\nIt's usually easier to understand a set if the elements are listed in some order.\n\n- *Duplicates don't matter.* Having multiple copies of an element does not affect the structure of a set. For example, the following sets represent the same thing:\n\n$$\nB = \\{m, \\: n, \\: p, \\: q\\} = \\{m, \\: m, \\: m, \\: n, \\: n, \\: p, \\: p, \\: p, \\: q\\}\n$$\n\nWhen writing a set, we should try to organize it so that each element is listed once only." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186056036 1064 "A **set** is a collection of objects known as **elements** or **members**.\n\nUsually, the elements of a set have some property in common. For example, a set could be a class at school where the students are the members of the set. The common property is that all the students are members of the class.\n\nTo write a set, we list the elements between two braces (curly brackets). Commas are used to separate the elements.\n\nSo, for example, the set of the primary colors would be written as\n\n$$\nS =\\{\\text{yellow}, \\: \\text{red}, \\: \\text{blue} \\}\n$$\n\nThe elements of this set are $\\text{yellow}$, $\\text{red}$, and $\\text{blue}$.\n\nA set that does not contain any elements is known as the **null set** or **empty set** and is written as either\n$\\emptyset \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\{\\}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- *A set may contain other sets as elements.* For instance, the set\n$T = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 4, \\: \\{5, 6\\}\\}$\ncontains the set $\\{5, 6\\}$ as an element. However, it does *not* contain the elements $5$ and $6$.\n- *Order doesn't matter.* The order in which the elements are listed is irrelevant. For instance, the following sets represent the same thing:\n$A = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3 \\} = \\{2, \\: 3, \\: 1\\}$\nIt's usually easier to understand a set if the elements are listed in some order.\n- *Duplicates don't matter.* Having multiple copies of an element does not affect the structure of a set. For example, the following sets represent the same thing:\n$B = \\{m, \\: n, \\: p, \\: q\\} = \\{m, \\: m, \\: m, \\: n, \\: n, \\: p, \\: p, \\: p, \\: q\\}$\nWhen writing a set, we should try to organize it so that each element is listed once only." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186056037 1064 "By convention, sets are denoted by uppercase letters, while lowercase letters refer to elements of the set.\n\nThere is a special notation, $\\in$, for expressing elements of sets. For example, if $a$ is an element of a set $A$, we write\n\n$$\na \\in A\n$$\n\nFor example, for the set $S$, given by\n\n$$\nS = \\{\\text{yellow}, \\text{red}, \\text{blue} \\}\n$$\n\nwe can write\n\n$$\n\\text{yellow}\\in S\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a$ is *not* an element of $A$, then we write\n\n$$\na \\notin A\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\text{green} \\notin S\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186056037 1064 "By convention, sets are denoted by uppercase letters, while lowercase letters refer to elements of the set.  \n\nThere is a special notation, $\\in,$ for expressing elements of sets. For example, if $a$ is an element of a set $A,$ we write\n\n$$\na \\in A.\n$$\n\nFor example, for the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\{ \\text{yellow}, \\text{red}, \\text{blue} \\},\n$$\n\nwe can write \n\n$$\n\\text{yellow}\\in S.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a$ is *not* an element of $A,$ then we write\n\n$$\na \\notin A.\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\text{green} \\notin S.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186056038 1064 "When a set contains only a few elements, we can write them all explicitly.\n\nFor example, the set $A$ containing all positive even integers less than $20$ could be written as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 10, \\: 12, \\: 14, \\: 16, \\: 18\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, when a set contains many elements, we can save ourselves the pain of writing out all its elements using an ellipsis, \"$\\ldots$\".\n\nFor example:\n\n- The set $B$ containing all positive even integers less than $200$ can be written as\n$B = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots, \\: 198\\}$.\n- The set $C$ containing *all* positive even integers can be written as\n$C = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots \\}$.\n\nGenerally, an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element or \"and so on\" if not.\n\nFinally, note that $A$ and $B$ contain a finite number of elements ($A$ and $B$ contain $9$ and $99$ elements, respectively), while $C$ contains infinitely many elements:\n\n- A set containing a finite number of elements is called a **finite set**.\n- A set containing an infinite number of elements is called an **infinite set**.\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ are finite sets, while $C$ is an infinite set." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186056038 1064 "When a set contains only a few elements, we can write them all explicitly. \n\nFor example, the set $A$ containing all positive even integers less than $20$ could be written as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 10, \\: 12, \\: 14, \\: 16, \\: 18\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, when a set contains many elements, we can save ourselves the pain of writing out all its elements using an ellipsis, \"$\\ldots$\". \n\nFor example:\n\n- The set $B$ containing all positive even integers less than $200$ can be written as\n\n$$\nB = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots, \\: 198\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ containing *all* positive even integers can be written as\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nGenerally, an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element or \"and so on\" if not.\n\nFinally, note that $A$ and $B$ contain a finite number of elements ($A$ and $B$ contain $9$ and $99$ elements, respectively), while $C$ cont