e power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sqrt{x}dx &= \\intx^{1/2}dx \\\\\n&= (x^{1/2 + 1})/((\\frac{1}{2} + 1)) + C \\\\\n&= (x^{3/2})/((\\frac{3}{2})) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3/2}) + 0 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x^{3/2 - 1} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{1/2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186064039 1074 "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186064040 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186064040 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186064040 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nthen integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF^{′}(x) &= f(x) \\\\\n\\intF^{′}(x)dx &= \\intf(x)dx \\\\\nF(x) &= \\intf(x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$