6045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106539 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106539 1071 "Find the derivative of $f(x) = \\frac{x^{2}}{\\sinh x}$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186106539 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106540 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106540 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $w(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106540 1071 "Find the derivative of $w(x) = e^{2x}\\cosh 2x$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186106540 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{2x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106541 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106541 1071 "$$\n\nIf $w(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x},$ then ${w}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot g(x)}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}.$ What is $g(x)?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106541 1071 "If $w(x) = \\frac{e^{x}}{\\tanh 2x}$, then $w^{′}(x) = (e^{x} \\cdot g(x))/(\\tanh^{2} 2x)$. What is $g(x)$?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186106541 1074 "In this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 2{sech}^{2}⁡2x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\overset{g(x)}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x)}}}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106542 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106542 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $y=-14\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106542 1071 "Find the derivative of $y =-14\\tanh x$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186106542 1074 "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-14\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-14\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-14{sech}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106543 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106543 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=4{sinh}^{3}⁡x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186106543 1071 "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 4\\sinh^{3} x$." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186106543 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=4{g}^{3},\\,g=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=12{g}^{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =12{g}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\  & =12{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186107119 1064 "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives.\n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\cosh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) &= \\sinh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x - 2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(-2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) - 2\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x - 2\\sinh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186107119 1064 "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives. \n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & ={sech}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186107120 1064 "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} %\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x})\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} - e^{-x})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x} - e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186107120 1064 "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n%$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x}).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186107121 1064 "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\sech^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{e^{2x} - 1}{e^{2x} + 1}) \\\\\n&= ((e^{2x} - 1)^{′} \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1) \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1)^{′})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2e^{2x})(e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1)(2e^{2x}))/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x} - 2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2e^{x}}{e^{2x} + 1})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2}{e^{x} + e^{-x}})^{2} \\\\\n&= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186107121 1064 "$\\n