xt{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186167287 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186167287 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186167287 1071 "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx)$\nLet $F(x) =-5x$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$. The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_{n}$.$a_{n} =$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n \\ge 1$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186167287 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{10(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186167431 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186167431 1071 "$$\n$\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186167431 1071 "What is $\\coth (\\ln 2)$?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186167431 1074 "$$\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+1}{{e}^{2x}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2x}}{1-{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the third definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}+1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186181248 1075 17592186181249 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186181248 1075 17592186181250 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186181248 1075 17592186513904 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186181248 1075 17592186513944 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186181248 1078 17592186513944 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186181254 1075 17592186181256 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186181254 1075 17592186181258 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186181254 1075 17592186513628 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186181254 1075 17592186514083 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186181254 1078 17592186181259 13194139535541 17