 of the data.\n\n![](images/c4/c47327219f03934832e62176db1441a94d9a78edda2f0d398a63b2942ec6a845.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.669 & 0.743 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.669\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.669 & 0.743 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.669\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2} & 0.743{x}_{1}-0.669{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186185303 1064 "Suppose we are analyzing house prices in a specific neighborhood, gathering data on quantitative features such as the total floor space, number of bedrooms and bathrooms, and distance to the town center. Our goal is to understand the relationship between these features and property values.\n\nProblems involving multi-dimensional data analysis, such as this one, pose several challenges:\n\n- Multi-dimensional data is often difficult to analyze.\n\n- Visualization can be virtually impossible.\n\n- Storing multi-dimensional data on a computer can require substantial hardware resources.\n\nWe can overcome these challenges using **dimensionality reduction.** Dimensionality reduction works by compressing data into a smaller number of variables while (ideally) preserving as much information as possible.\n\nTo demonstrate the basic idea behind dimensionality reduction, consider the following data set for the variables $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n![](images/8a/8a8d724370467974845492c885ee680473c48f983a28680613740305c353e701.png)\n\nNotice that the data does not vary much in the $y$-direction. Therefore, we can transform the data set by projecting all points onto the $x$-axis. This process removes the variation in $y$ with almost no loss of overall information, as shown below:\n\n![](images/3f/3f9acbf44838ebe6099b250bb60d88dcae970158d7db86230f597ecbf62c5d43.png)\n\nMulti-dimensional data typically exhibit characteristics suitable for dimensionality reduction:\n\n- Many variables are often correlated. In our house prices example, the number of bedrooms and bathrooms likely correlate. This means that one variable can often be expressed in terms of another. Or, we can find a new variable that describes the variation in both original variables.\n\n- Ultimately, we can aim to reduce the higher-dimensional structure of the data to a more fundamental, lower-dimensional structure containing only uncorrelated variables.\n\nBy reducing the dimensionality of the data, we can work with a compact representation that retains only the most critical variables and discards the rest. This simplifies analysis and visualization." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186185303 1064 "Suppose we are analyzing house prices in a specific neighborhood, gathering data on quantitative features such as the total floor space, number of bedrooms and bathrooms, and distance to the town center. Our goal is to understand the relationship between these features and property values.\n\nProblems involving multi-dimensional data analysis, such as this one, pose several challenges:\n\n- Multi-dimensional data is often difficult to analyze.\n- Visualization can be virtually impossible.\n- Storing multi-dimensional data on a computer can require substantial hardware resources.\n\nWe can overcome these challenges using **dimensionality reduction.** Dimensionality reduction works by compressing data into a smaller number of variables while (ideally) preserving as much information as possible.\n\nTo demonstrate the basic idea behind dimensionality reduction, consider the following data set for the variables $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n![](images/8a/8a8d724370467974845492c885ee680473c48f983a28680613740305c353e701.png)\n\nNotice that the data does not vary much in the $y$-direction. Therefore, we can transform the data set by projecting all points onto the $x$-axis. This process removes the variation in $y$ with almost no loss of overall information, as shown below:\n\n![](images/3f/3f9acbf44838ebe6099b250bb60d88dcae970158d7db86230f597ecbf62c5d43.png)\n\nMulti-dimensional data typically exhibit characteristics suitable for dimensionality reduction:\n\n- Many variables are often correlated. In our house prices example, the number of bedrooms and bathrooms likely correlate. This means that one variable can often be expressed in terms of another. Or, we can find a new variable that describes the variation in both original variables.\n- Ultimately, we can aim to reduce the higher-dimensional structure of the data to a more fundamental, lower-dimensional structure containing only uncorrelated variables.\n\nBy reducing the dimensionality of the data, we can work with a compact representation that retains only the most critical variables and discards the rest. This simplifies analysis and visualization." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186185304 1064 "In the previous section, we saw that dimensionality reduction compresses data into fewer, uncorrelated variables. **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)** is a widely used technique for achieving this.\n\nSuppose we have a sample of $n$ observations, each with $m$ features. We organize this data into an $n \\times m$ **observation matrix** $X$, where each row is an observation and each column is a feature.\n\nFor example, if we had $n=3$ observations of $m=2$ features, our observation matrix might look like this:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 20 \\\\ 3 & 30\\end{bmatrix}$\nBefore applying PCA, we must standardize the data. This is because PCA is sensitive to the scale of features. For instance, measuring a feature in meters versus centimeters would lead to different results. We standardize by giving each column a mean of $0$ and a standard deviation of $1$.\n\nFor our example, the first column has mean $2$ and standard deviation $1$, while the second column has mean $20$ and standard deviation $10$. Our standardized observation matrix $X$ is:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(1 - 2)/1 & (10 - 20)/10 \\\\ (2 - 2)/1 & (20 - 20)/10 \\\\ (3 - 2)/1 & (30 - 20)/10\\end{bmatrix}$\nNext, we'll use this standardized data to analyze the relationships between the features." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186185304 1064 "In the previous section, we saw that dimensionality reduction compresses data into fewer, uncorrelated variables. **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)** is a widely used technique for achieving this.\n\nSuppose we have a sample of $n$ observations, each with $m$ features. We organize this data into an $n \\times m$ **observation matrix** $X,$ where each row is an observation and each column is a feature.\n\nFor example, if we had $n=3$ observations of $m=2$ features, our observation matrix might look like this:\n![](images/ac/ac2f4b137b2c63734a7ce5f03f42c099b12aa62bb74a4345a948d512bc378c0e.svg)\nBefore applying PCA, we must standardize the data. This is because PCA is sensitive to the scale of features. For instance, measuring a feature in meters versus centimeters would lead to different results. We standardize by giving each column a mean of $0$ and a standard deviation of $1.$\n\nFor our example, the first column has mean $2$ and standard deviation $1,$ while the second column has mean $20$ and standard deviation $10.$ Our standardized observation matrix $X$ is:\n![](images/dc/dcb9c2bda09dbc8417c65d4345467a5eb2b580c61b77679c5273909d59cc6727.svg)\nNext, we'll use this standardized data to analyze the relationships between the features." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186185305 1064 "The statistical interpretation of the principal components is as follows:\n\n- The $1$st principal component defines a direction along which the original data has the maximum variance.\n- The $2$nd principal component is perpendicular to the $1$st component and defines a direction along which the remaining data has the maximum variance.\n- The $3$rd principal component is perpendicular to both the $1$st and the $2$nd components and defines a direction along which the remaining data has the maximum variance.$\\vdots$ and so on.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following scatter plot of a sample of two-dimensional (paired) observations.\n\n![](images/b9/b9db4d78847162a66cb3541387c9fed32ce7f717e069d9a6d6a7135bd87eda3f.png)\n\nIn our diagram, the direction along which the data varies the most corresponds (approximately) to the line shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1cab88d6f754736ec8a2f8c61861d9e5d24193b376dc6deed2591e44a75a8b72.png)\n\nTherefore, the vectors corresponding to the first and second principal components of the data could be the unit vectors shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b495503b421f2f1190595167bc031821d31b80b6bb95e0595bd8a7df41a60a1d.png)" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186185305 1064 "The statistical interpretation of the principal components is as follows: \n\n- The $1$st principal component defines a direction along which the original data has the maximum variance.\n\n- The $2$nd principal component is perpendicular to the $1$st component and defines a direction along which the remaining data has the maximum variance.\n\n- The $3$rd principal component is perpendicular to both the $1$st and the $2$nd components and defines a direction along which the remaining data has the maximum variance.\n\n$\\vdots$ \n\nand so on.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following scatter plot of a sample of two-dimensional (paired) observations.  \n\n![](images/b9/b9db4d78847162a66cb3541387c9fed32ce7f717e069d9a6d6a7135bd87eda3f.png)\n\n \n\nIn our diagram, the direction along which the data varies the most corresponds (approximately) to the line shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1cab88d6f754736ec8a2f8c61861d9e5d24193b376dc6deed2591e44a75a8b72.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vectors corresponding to the first and second principal components of the data could be the unit vectors shown below. \n\n![](images/b4/b495503b421f2f1190595167bc031821d31b80b6bb95e0595bd8a7df41a60a1d.png)" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186185306 1064 "Suppose a physician measured two features, $x_1$ and $x_2$, corresponding to height and weight for each athlete among a group who attended a particular competition. The collected data were stored in a standardized observation matrix, with each row containing both features for each athlete.\n\nA scatter plot of the physician's results is shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df1dab6868232dd53da67b881c7de11cfd47cd84abe35ba94540e5ce038e9d1e.png)\n\nRemember that the first and second principal components, denoted $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$, are the unit eigenvectors of the sample covariance matrix for the data set. The physician calculates that the first and second principal components of this data are approximately\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.316 \\\\ 0.947\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe first and second principal components are shown below:\n\n![](images/65/65fdad4f8a1d1a7df8f724f6549d183b4b2800eb1cbb3fd45275949e8a8d0199.png)\n\nThe physician wants to combine the two features linearly into a single feature that preserves the maximum variance. What is the best candidate for the new feature?\n\nThe matrix of principal components, denoted $V$, is given by\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; v_{1} \\mid v_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables $Y = XV,\\,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are uncorrelated), and\n- the features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in $y_1$).\n\nApplying the change of variables to a single observation $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}V \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.316 & -0.947 \\\\ 0.947 & 0.316\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.316x_{1} + 0.947x_{2} & -0.947x_{1} + 0.316x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.316 x_1 + 0.947 x_2\n$$\n\nwill contain most of the variance of the data. Therefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.316 x_1 + 0.947 x_2\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186185306 1064 "Suppose a physician measured two features, $x_1$ and $x_2,$ corresponding to height and weight for each athlete among a group who attended a particular competition. The collected data were stored in a standardized observation matrix, with each row containing both features for each athlete.\n\nA scatter plot of the physician's results is shown below. \n\n![](images/df/df1dab6868232dd53da67b881c7de11cfd47cd84abe35ba94540e5ce038e9d1e.png)\n\n \n\nRemember that the first and second principal components, denoted $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2,$ are the unit eigenvectors of the sample covariance matrix for the data set. The physician calculates that the first and second principal components of this data are approximately \n![](images/a9/a9ff20e8b2cbd8caad2bcf79f2a6fd369b9788a939108c6d0d99aa6654521b79.svg)\n\nThe first and second principal components are shown below:\n\n![](images/65/65fdad4f8a1d1a7df8f724f6549d183b4b2800eb1cbb3fd45275949e8a8d0199.png)\n\n \n\nThe physician wants to combine the two features linearly into a single feature that preserves the maximum variance. What is the best candidate for the new feature?\n\nThe matrix of principal components, denoted $V,$ is given by\n\n![](images/10/108e96292ad3af7d2d4a171925fd3554ed424e5f41b47eaeced4617affbdb695.svg)\n\nApplying the change of variables $Y = XV,\\,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in $y_1$).\n\nApplying the change of variables to a single observation ![](images/17/170f8ecc20d8fab03e5a15749aefe7efa71b08671f99276a1261961040333185.svg) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.316 & -0.947 \\\\ 0.947 & 0.316\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.316{x}_{1}+0.947{x}_{2} & -0.947{x}_{1}+0.316{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $y_1 = 0.316 x_1 + 0.947 x_2$ will contain most of the variance of the data. Therefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.316 x_1 + 0.947 x_2.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186185307 1064 "Recall that our goal is to find new features that are uncorrelated — equivalently, features whose covariance matrix is diagonal. In the previous section, we found an orthogonal matrix $V$ such that $V^T\\!CV$ is diagonal. This motivates defining a **transformed observation matrix** $Y = XV$. The columns of this new matrix $Y$ are our new features.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} X^T X\n$$\n\ninto the expression $V^T\\!CV$ and noting that\n\n$$\n(XV)^T = V^T X^T{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV^{T}CV_(⏟)_(diagonal) &= V^{T}(\\frac{1}{n - 1}X^{T}X)V \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}(XV)^{T}(XV) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}Y^{T}Y_(⏟)_(covariance of [math]Y[/math])\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal, so the new features $y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_m$ are uncorrelated. The diagonal entries are the eigenvalues of $C$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(y_i) = \\lambda_i\n$$\n\nBy arranging the columns of $V$ in order of decreasing eigenvalue, the new features are also arranged in order of decreasing variance.\n\nThe columns of $V$ (the unit eigenvectors of $C$) are called the **principal components**. The $1$st *principal component* is the eigenvector corresponding to the largest eigenvalue, the $2$nd corresponds to the second-largest, and so on.\n\nThis procedure is the core of **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)**. To summarize:\n\n> *We define the change-of-variables $Y = XV$, where the columns of $V$ are the **principal components** of the data, arranged in order of decreasing eigenvalue. This ensures that:*\n> - *The new features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m$ are uncorrelated (diagonal covariance).*\n> - *The new features are arranged in order of decreasing variance ($\\text{Var}(y_i) = \\lambda_i$).*\n\nNow let's understand what these vectors mean in terms of the original data." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186185307 1064 "Recall that our goal is to find new features that are uncorrelated — equivalently, features whose covariance matrix is diagonal. In the previous section, we found an orthogonal matrix $V$ such that $V^T\\!CV$ is diagonal. This motivates defining a **transformed observation matrix** $Y = XV.$ The columns of this new matrix $Y$ are our new features.\n\nSubstituting $C = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} X^T X$ into the expression $V^T\\!CV$ and noting that $(XV)^T = V^T X^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{V}^{T}\\,CV}_{⏟}}_{\\text{diagonal}} & ={V}^{T}(\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X)V \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(XV)^{T}(XV) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{n-1}{Y}^{T}Y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{covariance of [math]Y[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal, so the new features $y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_m$ are uncorrelated. The diagonal entries are the eigenvalues of $C,$ so $\\text{Var}(y_i) = \\lambda_i.$ By arranging the columns of $V$ in order of decreasing eigenvalue, the new features are also arranged in order of decreasing variance.\n\nThe columns of $V$ (the unit eigenvectors of $C$) are called the **principal components**. The $1$st *principal component* is the eigenvector corresponding to the largest eigenvalue, the $2$nd corresponds to the second-largest, and so on.\n\nThis procedure is the core of **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)**. To summarize:\n\n*We define the change-of-variables $Y = XV,$ where the columns of $V$ are the **principal components** of the data, arranged in order of decreasing eigenvalue. This ensures that:*\n\n- *The new features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m$ are uncorrelated (diagonal covariance).*\n\n- *The new features are arranged in order of decreasing variance ($\\text{Var}(y_i) = \\lambda_i$).*\n\nNow let's understand what these vectors mean in terms of the original data." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186185308 1064 "Let's look again at the covariance matrix we just calculated:\n![](images/e5/e5359936cd43c32f4c937effa775f6d65527dbd1e14882b25ecef7b31afd7515.svg)\nNotice that the matrix $C$ is symmetric. In fact, the sample covariance matrix is *always* a real symmetric matrix.\n\nRecall from linear algebra that any real symmetric matrix is **orthogonally diagonalizable**. This means we can find an orthogonal matrix $V$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ such that\n\n$$\nV^T C V = D\n$$\n\nThe entries of the diagonal matrix $D$ are the eigenvalues of $C,$ and the columns of the matrix $V$ are the corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors.\n\nNext, we shall see how to use the matrix $V$ to find uncorrelated features for our data." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186185308 1064 "Let's look again at the covariance matrix we just calculated:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nNotice that the matrix $C$ is symmetric. In fact, the sample covariance matrix is *always* a real symmetric matrix.\n\nRecall from linear algebra that any real symmetric matrix is **orthogonally diagonalizable**. This means we can find an orthogonal matrix $V$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ such that\n$V^T C V = D$\nThe entries of the diagonal matrix $D$ are the eigenvalues of $C$, and the columns of the matrix $V$ are the corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors.\n\nNext, we shall see how to use the matrix $V$ to find uncorrelated features for our data." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186185309 1064 "Now that our data matrix $X$ is standardized, we examine the relationships between its features using the **sample covariance matrix**, $C$. For standardized data, this is calculated as:\n\n$$\nC=\\dfrac{1}{n-1} X^T X\n$$\n\nUsing our standardized matrix from the previous slide, where $n=3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= \\frac{1}{3 - 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal entries of $C$ are the variances of the features, and the off-diagonal entries are the covariances between pairs of features." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186185309 1064 "Now that our data matrix $X$ is standardized, we examine the relationships between its features using the **sample covariance matrix**, $C.$ For standardized data, this is calculated as:\n\n$$\nC=\\dfrac{1}{n-1} X^T X\n$$\n\nUsing our standardized matrix from the previous slide, where $n=3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal entries of $C$ are the variances of the features, and the off-diagonal entries are the covariances between pairs of features." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189676 1075 17592186189677 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189676 1075 17592186189678 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189676 1075 17592186189679 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189676 1075 17592186189680 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189676 1075 17592186189681 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189676 1075 17592186513609 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189676 1075 17592186513724 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189676 1075 17592186514025 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189676 1075 17592186514073 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189676 1075 17592186514090 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189676 1078 17592186514090 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189688 1075 17592186189689 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189688 1075 17592186189690 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189688 1075 17592186189691 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189688 1075 17592186189692 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189688 1075 17592186189693 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186189688 1075 17592186513569 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189688 1075 17592186513740 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186189688 1075 1759218651384