{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186193823 1064 "Recall that the principal components of a standardized observation matrix $X$ are the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then:\n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1$,\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nFor example, suppose that the covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C$, in descending order, are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10, \\qquad \\lambda_2=8\n$$\n\nTo find the first principal component of the data, we need the unit eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10\n$$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC - 10I &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix} - 10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, seeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(C-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st principal component of the data is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The opposite vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ also works in the example above." 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186193823 1064 "Recall that the principal components of a standardized observation matrix $X$ are the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$ If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nFor example, suppose that the covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is \n\n![](images/e4/e40cd1a0df89ba171b921ab971e8eee25e284265df13b7e031ea32fc28d2c27b.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show that the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the first principal component of the data, we need the unit eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=10.$ \n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, seeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/c7/c7050bbb395b939b1b0559b3e7515c4be4f572aed61090f1a4489e126763240f.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n![](images/7b/7b0a41deb385aa2471cae701c9f87938085256879a8d834535f8ec0e3f431f27.svg) \n\nTherefore, the $1$st principal component of the data is ![](images/94/9490f2e5105592fa3976ae506cbdf64d70c3372d1cc9295eaa6c6580a4ac75aa.svg)\n\n**Note:** The opposite vector ![](images/e2/e2b366cfb8cb5b708eb5ba443dfc61c05b498d4eb737b375c8ebc86b96e8eea6.svg) also works in the example above." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186198442 1038 17592186514484 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186233026 1068 17592186514299 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186233174 1068 17592186514278 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186233588 1068 17592186514230 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186233686 1068 17592186514269 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186233993 1068 17592186514296 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186233999 1068 17592186514315 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186234299 1068 17592186514344 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186234299 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186234299 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186234299 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186234299 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186314636 1075 17592186513906 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186314636 1078 17592186313840 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186314636 1078 17592186513906 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186314680 1075 17592186513999 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186314680 1078 17592186312273 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186314680 1078 17592186513999 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186370186 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186370186 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186370186 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3xy=0 \\\\ 7y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3xy & =0 \\\\ x(-2+3y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{2}{3},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(7-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(7-5x) & =0 \\\\ 7-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{2}{3}).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186370186 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3xy=0 \\\\ 7y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3xy & =0 \\\\ x(-2+3y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{2}{3},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(7-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(7-5x) & =0 \\\\ 7-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{2}{3}).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 false] [17592186495042 1038 17592186514453 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186495042 1038 17592186514481 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186495043 1038 17592186514380 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186495043 1038 17592186514418 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186495046 1038 17592186514462 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186513513 1043 17592186514520 13194139535541 17592186045417 tr