2186050902 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514355 1089 17592186514356 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514355 1090 #uuid "d0365b9e-246c-5835-9ec1-5b3386ca8f07" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514491 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514493 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514498 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514501 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514502 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514506 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514508 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514509 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514511 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1084 17592186514544 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1085 "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1086 #uuid "c918a931-a94f-57cf-9983-07568d2db58a" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514356 1087 17592186514491 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514357 1068 17592186514298 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514357 1069 "q-114096" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514357 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514357 1071 "Which of the following is **NOT** a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-21x+45?$\n\nI. $x=3$\n\nII. $x=4$\n\nIII. $x=5$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514357 1072 #uuid "01d0ba8c-f3fb-52e6-bc68-8e1659694309" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514357 1074 "- Substituting $3$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}-{3}^{2}-21(3)+45 & = \\\\ 27-9-63+45 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $3$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3}-{4}^{2}-21(4)+45 & = \\\\ 64-16-84+45 & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3}-{5}^{2}-21(5)+45 & = \\\\ 125-25-105+45 & = \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $5$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the values that are *not* roots are $x=4$ and $x=5,$ so the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514358 1068 17592186514231 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514358 1069 "q-125371" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514358 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514358 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-5)=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514358 1072 #uuid "0325034f-c697-5e84-82d0-1d5908709799" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514358 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-5) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-5}-{e}^{-(-5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-5}-{e}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514359 1068 17592186514294 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514359 1069 "q-2982" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514359 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514359 1071 "${2}^{x}=64$ can be expressed as" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514359 1072 #uuid "053acc10-5279-5930-8cdc-4b0bb8c6548c" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514359 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${2}^{x}=64$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=64$ and $b=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${2}^{x}=64$ is equivalent to ${log}_{2}⁡64=x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514360 1068 17592186514288 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514360 1069 "q-95670" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514360 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514360 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $y=6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514360 1072 #uuid "05b36d37-a26f-5d16-ae0b-dd50ba7890e6" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514360 1074 "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{sech}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514361 1068 17592186514328 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514361 1069 "q-66551" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514361 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514361 1071 "${2}^{x}=32$ is equivalent to" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514361 1072 #uuid "084b231a-35a2-58f7-86fa-95eb22a6b762" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514361 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${2}^{x}=32$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=32$ and $b=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${2}^{x}=32$ is equivalent to ${log}_{2}⁡32=x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514362 1068 17592186514236 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514362 1068 17592186514335 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514362 1069 "q-328385" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514362 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514362 1071 "The center of the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-10x-16y=-5$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514362 1072 #uuid "087134b9-662e-5552-a87e-db77c3a63f60" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514362 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-10x-16y & =-5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-10x)-4({y}^{2}+4y) & =-5 \\\\ [(x-5)^{2}-25]-4[(y+2)^{2}-4] & =-5 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-25-4(y+2)^{2}+16 & =-5 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-4(y+2)^{2} & =-5+25-16 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-4(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,5,-2\\,).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514363 1068 17592186514270 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514363 1069 "q-149392" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514363 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514363 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514363 1072 #uuid "09aebc44-422b-52f7-a02d-eaf1743e7ebe" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514363 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514364 1068 17592186514338 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514364 1069 "q-148479" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514364 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514364 1071 "![](images/cb/cb757f7a8b81147203ca1e58f040f4327f5f4c92971b9e0f1cf11f1c4fadcdfb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514364 1072 #uuid "09d173fe-eece-5b8b-9031-7816fb223b07" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514364 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $b$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb62100b65cf97e42cce7e1ce4dc0923fa291f4684b720369b1cdbd21a4393fd.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ and $c$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514365 1068 17592186514285 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514365 1069 "q-125697" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514365 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514365 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $t\\ne 0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,t)?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514365 1072 #uuid "0af3be74-c5b3-5ec8-af78-1603fe65ae67" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514365 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\,t)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\sqrt{7}\\,t}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{7}\\,t}}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514366 1068 17592186514259 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514366 1069 "q-34924" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514366 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514366 1071 "![](images/f8/f8b33f31c4b569da24aa8235421698859e74b144e783f0fb917a5f4b3679d834.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514366 1072 #uuid "0bebd627-ffbe-5f9e-9279-43f33938aacb" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514366 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514367 1068 17592186514254 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514367 1069 "q-80294" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514367 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514367 1071 "Which set represents all positive integers less than or equal to $15?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514367 1072 #uuid "101cf0dd-9e7d-5358-b3ec-977545014b49" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514367 1074 "To express the desired set, we start with the first positive integer less than or equal to $15,$ which is $1.$ The next positive integer is $2,$ the one after that is $3,$ and so on up to $15$ (which is the last number that is less than or equal to $15$).\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\{1,2,3,…,15\\}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514368 1068 17592186514247 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514368 1069 "q-2591" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514368 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514368 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{4a-6b+9c}{3}?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514368 1072 #uuid "1169d5be-77b9-5c01-b29e-d873231e9f86" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514368 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4a-6b+9c}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-\\frac{6b}{3}+\\frac{9c}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-\\frac{6}{3}\\cdot b+\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot c & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-2b+3c & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514369 1068 17592186514244 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514369 1069 "q-42998" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514369 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514369 1071 "Find $det({A}^{-1})$, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514369 1072 #uuid "13976613-adec-5296-be7c-cccd907c4b03" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514369 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514370 1068 17592186514245 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514370 1069 "q-77741" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514370 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514370 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{10x-3}}{{x}^{2}-3}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514370 1072 #uuid "156d5c20-d8d9-52c6-bf0a-a41e2c4bb6cd" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514370 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{10x-3}}{{x}^{2}-3}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\sqrt[3]{10x-3})}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-3)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\sqrt[3]{10x-3}) & =\\sqrt[3]{10(3)-3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{30-3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-3) & =(3)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{10x-3}}{{x}^{2}-3} & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514371 1068 17592186514265 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514371 1069 "q-67531" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514371 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514371 1071 "![](images/a4/a45bf16dabedfdd0c8f4569289c56a5f89c127e240f57a8b9259429be86a9cde.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514371 1072 #uuid "1580d634-b7c9-566e-bd94-0e3785439c42" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514371 1074 "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514372 1068 17592186514240 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514372 1069 "q-95671" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514372 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514372 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514372 1072 #uuid "16fea3d9-04d8-53ba-a81e-4ab7949c02d8" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514372 1074 "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514373 1068 17592186514307 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514373 1069 "q-103607" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514373 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514373 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{12-3y}{6}?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514373 1072 #uuid "18e51362-5c55-557f-b76e-006c8ec5e5f2" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514373 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-3y}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{3y}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514374 1068 17592186514237 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514374 1069 "q-96531" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514374 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514374 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514374 1072 #uuid "196ecf31-b7aa-5c38-b760-a54c4f34d305" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514374 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{-2}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{-2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{4}-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{15}{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514375 1068 17592186514221 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514375 1069 "q-149404" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514375 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514375 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $4,$ and $8.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514375 1072 #uuid "1dfd23ff-dd1b-5e4d-b783-10b5e0f038d4" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514375 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514376 1068 17592186514246 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514376 1069 "q-148516" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514376 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514376 1071 "![](images/6b/6bc83d9194c0d6728e875d4089c97eb92ea45d3d2ef735f882dafe3991b9d2ec.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514376 1072 #uuid "1e02af7d-5317-58cc-b4e8-ba89b18e708e" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514376 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $a$ or $c,$ as shown below. So, either $a$ or $c$ define the direction of the first principal component.  \n\n![](images/dd/dd4ca330f3e37334133a6cde00220b872a34afe3bf9d8c5c76f37111c30b8291.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab3d8561432f3862503eb4b213347d11dc2158deeade50a6418867b6f2b03dc3.png)\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514377 1068 17592186514309 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514377 1069 "q-300672" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514377 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514377 1071 "$$\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{sech}⁡7.$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n$\\operatorname{sech}⁡7=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514377 1072 #uuid "221afffc-6058-53ae-8f4b-ca54ee801c93" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514377 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡7 & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{7}+{e}^{-7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514378 1068 17592186514333 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514378 1069 "q-344084" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514378 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514378 1071 "A neuroscientist measured two specific features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each brain image of a group of patients he is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular brain image. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ -0.707\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the neuroscientist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514378 1072 #uuid "2381e2c6-90e5-5e9a-8022-1f6b70cb4895" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514378 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/70/70a37afaaad54e3f384ad446ab2cf57bc0f21ffa8f6986f01841d9f4041f531c.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2} & -0.707{x}_{1}+0.707{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514379 1068 17592186514218 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514379 1069 "q-250309" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514379 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514379 1071 "Evaluate the following expression. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$-5\\,\\text{i}-8\\,\\text{i}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514379 1072 #uuid "246ccde2-7d9b-5b08-9b39-f9d20f01227f" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514379 1074 "To add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nIn this case, $-5\\,\\text{i}$ and $8\\,\\text{i}$ are purely imaginary numbers. Therefore, we only need to add their imaginary parts, as follows:\n\n$$\n-5\\,\\text{i}-8\\,\\text{i}=(-5-8)\\,\\text{i}=-13\\,\\text{i}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514380 1068 17592186514217 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514380 1069 "q-355712" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514380 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514380 1071 "At the deli, the unit price of specialty cheese is $$2$ per ounce. How many ounces of specialty cheese can Rohan buy for $$1?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514380 1072 #uuid "25c7031c-7a26-5c4d-b79f-0063afe0df6f" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514380 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$2$ per ounce (dollars per ounce). To find how many ounces of specialty cheese he can buy for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in ounces per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n2\\,\\text{dollars per ounce}\\,\\to \\,1÷2=0.5\\,\\text{ounces per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.5$ ounces per dollar. Therefore, Rohan can buy $0.5$ ounces of specialty cheese for $1$ dollar." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514381 1068 17592186514323 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514381 1069 "q-341279" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514381 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514381 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=8{x}^{2}+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514381 1072 #uuid "26db2697-717d-50e6-ae97-51a48de209b3" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514381 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=8{x}^{2}+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(8{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+3x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{8{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+3\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514382 1068 17592186514330 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514382 1069 "q-108051" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514382 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514382 1071 "${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{-3}=\\frac{27}{125}$ can be expressed as" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514382 1072 #uuid "277ed942-8515-5af5-8070-395ac017b6ed" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514382 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{-3}=\\frac{27}{125}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{27}{125},b=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${log}_{5/3}⁡(\\frac{27}{125})=-3.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514383 1068 17592186514337 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514383 1069 "q-29993" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514383 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514383 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514383 1072 #uuid "28f4ccc6-88e6-5461-934d-fc36db9c8ad4" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514383 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514384 1068 17592186514277 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514384 1069 "q-166078" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514384 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514384 1071 "$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514384 1072 #uuid "2932e26e-2b57-549a-866d-7abe171c7f20" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514384 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514385 1068 17592186514312 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514385 1069 "q-166076" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514385 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514385 1071 "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514385 1072 #uuid "2d877a64-29c8-5e32-8920-d62abb73e697" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514385 1074 "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514386 1068 17592186514229 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514386 1069 "q-149430" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514386 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514386 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $3,$ and $9.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514386 1072 #uuid "30659c57-f541-5ec5-857d-e24511eb8172" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514386 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514387 1068 17592186514264 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514387 1069 "q-340827" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514387 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514387 1071 "$$\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\sqrt{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n$\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\sqrt{5})=$\n {{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514387 1072 #uuid "3203ea85-5b81-5f7d-8926-d8feaf4a8223" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514387 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+1}{{e}^{2x}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2x}}{1-{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the third definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\sqrt{5})=\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{5}}+1}{{e}^{2\\sqrt{5}}-1}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514388 1068 17592186514255 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514388 1069 "q-2024" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514388 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514388 1071 "$\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514388 1072 #uuid "34621fcb-1a0d-5d3e-9880-09e09830a08f" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514388 1074 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514389 1068 17592186514350 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514389 1069 "q-142722" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514389 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514389 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{15},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.02.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514389 1072 #uuid "3619ef06-3622-5375-8459-4e89f07dd7d1" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514389 1074 "![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.02$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.02\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.01.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{15},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=15.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.567$ | $2.898$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.602)=0.010.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.602.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514390 1068 17592186514224 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514390 1069 "q-29665" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514390 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514390 1071 "$\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514390 1072 #uuid "37cc1eff-0240-5120-ba5e-14ada8bc6fca" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514390 1074 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{(\\frac{5}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514391 1068 17592186514303 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514391 1069 "q-113703" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514391 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514391 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514391 1072 #uuid "3ce88619-593c-543d-be7b-78867655bb11" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514391 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514392 1068 17592186514243 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514392 1069 "q-66569" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514392 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514392 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{4}≈1.587$ is equivalent to" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514392 1072 #uuid "3dd9e34f-3ee0-5a42-8a59-8773ae3a745a" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514392 1074 "Notice that the given approximation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{4}^{1/3}≈1.587\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${4}^{1/3}≈1.587$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=1.587,b=4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the approximation $\\sqrt[3]{4}≈1.587$ is equivalent to ${log}_{4}⁡(1.587)≈\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514393 1068 17592186514332 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514393 1069 "q-148476" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514393 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514393 1071 "![](images/6b/6bc83d9194c0d6728e875d4089c97eb92ea45d3d2ef735f882dafe3991b9d2ec.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514393 1072 #uuid "3eab3230-c0ce-5edb-86e7-28c4e5295d2f" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514393 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $a$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98ccbf23301a3c150409103a4f4a80534acadc365d4335e7f70435cd4d70fde0.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a$ and $c$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514394 1068 17592186514250 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514394 1068 17592186514327 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514394 1069 "q-218434" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514394 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514394 1071 "Expressed as a complex number,\n\n$(1+\\sqrt{-45})-(-5+\\sqrt{-20})=$\n{{field-1}}$+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514394 1072 #uuid "40969b77-0e08-50d1-86b5-46f58976876a" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514394 1074 "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\sqrt{-45})-(-5+\\sqrt{-20}) & =(1+\\sqrt{9\\cdot 5}\\,\\text{i})-(-5+\\sqrt{4\\cdot 5}\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(1+3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})-(-5+2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(1-(-5))+(3\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5})\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =(1+5)+\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =6+\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514395 1068 17592186514342 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514395 1069 "q-218423" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514395 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514395 1071 "Evaluate the following expression. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$(-5+12\\,\\text{i})+(6-9\\,\\text{i})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514395 1072 #uuid "43125a76-2814-5690-9f8b-34c658dd4ad3" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514395 1074 "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5+12\\,\\text{i})+(6-9\\,\\text{i}) & =(-5+6)+(12+(-9))\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =(-5+6)+(12-9)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =1+3\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514396 1068 17592186514301 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514396 1069 "q-95679" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514396 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514396 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514396 1072 #uuid "443677a2-c964-52f1-8cf6-218af419b0bb" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514396 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=\\sqrt{g},\\,g=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514397 1068 17592186514343 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514397 1069 "q-148480" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514397 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514397 1071 "![](images/79/79bae249d69216842f29df29c6b661d2a8e3aa684300bc6e15433d8a16fb5006.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514397 1072 #uuid "47ab5bde-d639-547f-ad10-9ccc080ffe38" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514397 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $a,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15d7060e172c87e42a6c69aa90e8f1cdfa649bdf21478f498b7b159f82aa8a68.png)\n\n \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514398 1068 17592186514317 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514398 1069 "q-165764" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514398 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514398 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)={e}^{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514398 1072 #uuid "49c81891-b661-5bda-b31c-54f1cc0220f3" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514398 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={e}^{g},\\,g=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡s,\\,s={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}s}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}={e}^{g},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}s}={sech}^{2}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the three results together and substituting for $g$ and $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{g}\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡s\\cdot 2x \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}}\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡{x}^{2}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{sech}^{2}⁡{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514399 1068 17592186514286 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514399 1069 "q-100306" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514399 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514399 1071 "![](images/b1/b12ba04b803d58c6cacf4fc781db5c7032c3aedad80506c101bf084109ebd797.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514399 1072 #uuid "522ad648-b326-5d90-9d3d-5351c0a03cfc" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514399 1074 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to -2}(\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)^{3}-3(-2)^{2}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-4-12}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514400 1068 17592186514238 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514400 1069 "q-109877" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514400 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514400 1071 "Consider a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=2,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514400 1072 #uuid "5241b6a1-96aa-5720-ba1b-4d884df832da" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514400 1074 "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=3,$ $n=2,$ and $r=2.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) & =n-r=2-2=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514401 1068 17592186514223 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514401 1069 "q-148518" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514401 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514401 1071 "![](images/9f/9f4f2f9c00a83968fb22d5fc22523db760bec0f903c692150683fbdb853f5027.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514401 1072 #uuid "525ecf36-0f83-5c22-a5cd-0246fa04eb42" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514401 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $c,$ as shown below. So, $c$ defines the direction of the first principal component.\n\n![](images/de/deb970bb184d902257c8610f7de8135abc2ba0461c614fa6508dbe1995964a2f.png)\n\n   \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/1a/1aaf90a34611401b2b4786b29077f192836aad060b6aca69764f165e20857217.png)\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514402 1068 17592186514249 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514402 1069 "q-138531" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514402 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514402 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $2.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514402 1072 #uuid "5374b353-43b9-56d5-bf37-798eca076a47" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514402 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514403 1068 17592186514292 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514403 1069 "q-142580" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514403 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514403 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{25},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.995.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514403 1072 #uuid "53bedfc9-6dd3-5f58-8bd9-2e76db557744" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514403 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X\\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.995 \\\\  & =0.005\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{25},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=25.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.787)=0.005.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.787.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514404 1068 17592186514272 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514404 1069 "q-125700" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514404 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514404 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $z>0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514404 1072 #uuid "558ac263-8cd5-56f3-9f79-c76511bfad24" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514404 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{z+(z)^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(z+\\frac{1}{z})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{{z}^{2}+1}{z})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514405 1068 17592186514351 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514405 1069 "q-142543" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514405 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514405 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{20},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.99.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514405 1072 #uuid "5c576b08-9f07-530e-b539-76adf5c7de7d" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514405 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X\\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.99 \\\\  & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{20},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=20.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.528)=0.010.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.528.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514406 1068 17592186514339 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514406 1069 "q-165768" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514406 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514406 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $t(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{3}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514406 1072 #uuid "5d5258aa-0eff-5685-bbfc-832665398ac8" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514406 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{4}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{3}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{3})^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}{sech}^{2}⁡{x}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{3}+3{x}^{6}{sech}^{2}⁡{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(4\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{3}+3{x}^{3}{sech}^{2}⁡{x}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514407 1068 17592186514239 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514407 1069 "q-138521" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514407 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514407 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $5,$ $4,$ and $2.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514407 1072 #uuid "5ddc6be0-7f0e-552b-88a6-224cbf5ee88c" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514407 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514408 1068 17592186514233 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514408 1069 "q-95273" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514408 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514408 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{2}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514408 1072 #uuid "5de32c1d-3413-5361-b37c-92eba300a033" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514408 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{2}}{x}+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514409 1068 17592186514287 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514409 1069 "q-166072" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514409 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514409 1071 "${g}^{″}(x)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514409 1072 #uuid "60d8ee2b-aed9-5a87-bc1d-99d095c196bb" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514409 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514410 1068 17592186514281 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514410 1069 "q-95319" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514410 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514410 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514410 1072 #uuid "620df981-8ac1-59e8-bd3d-58c107521764" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514410 1074 "In this case, the expression for $g(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514411 1068 17592186514275 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514411 1069 "q-109732" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514411 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514411 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(5,3)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(8,4)$ are independent and $Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y<14).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514411 1072 #uuid "65fa3d9f-f374-57d7-bfef-bf8f42da2e6e" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514411 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2} & ∼N(4{\\mu }_{1}-2{\\mu }_{2},16{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+4{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20-16,48+16) \\\\  & ∼N(4,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =4$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{64}=8.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<14).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<14) & =P(Z<\\frac{14-4}{8}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.25)≈0.8944.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<14)≈0.89.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514412 1068 17592186514313 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514412 1069 "q-109755" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514412 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514412 1071 "A factory produces shovels, each consisting of a handle and a blade.\nThe length of a handle is normally distributed with a mean of $120$ centimeters and a variance of $0.14,$  while the length of a blade is normally distributed with a mean of $30$ centimeters and a variance of $0.02.$ If one shovel is randomly selected, what is the probability that its length is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters? You may assume that the lengths are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514412 1072 #uuid "666db65e-d2dd-5df6-aa61-3e935a25f26e" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514412 1074 "Let $X$ denote the length of a handle, and let $Y$ denote the length of a blade. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(150<X+Y<150.5).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(120,0.14)$ and $Y∼N(30,0.02)$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(120+30,0.14+0.02) \\\\  & ∼N(150,0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =150$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.16}=0.4.$\n\nIn order to find $P(150<X+Y<150.5),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & =P(\\frac{150-150}{0.4}<Z<\\frac{150.5-150}{0.4}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<1.25) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.25)-P(Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.25) & ≈0.8944,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & ≈Φ(1.25)-Φ(0) \\\\  & ≈0.8944-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.3944.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the length of a randomly selected shovel is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters is $0.3944.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514413 1068 17592186514225 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514413 1068 17592186514352 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514413 1069 "q-250273" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514413 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514413 1071 "Expressed as a complex number,\n\n$(11-\\sqrt{-80})-(6+\\sqrt{-125})=$\n{{field-1}}$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514413 1072 #uuid "683bbf9d-fe44-596b-9cba-6b07323bd6d5" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514413 1074 "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(11-\\sqrt{-80})-(6+\\sqrt{-125}) & =(11-\\sqrt{16\\cdot 5}\\,\\text{i})-(6+\\sqrt{25\\cdot 5}\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(11-4\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})-(6+5\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(11-6)+(-4\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5})\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =5-9\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514414 1068 17592186514319 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514414 1069 "q-104392" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514414 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514414 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a set?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514414 1072 #uuid "68a9ef69-1a68-5a42-bd02-0ed93671db30" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514414 1074 "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $∅.$\n\n- $\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* a set. The numbers $\\frac{2}{7},$ $\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\frac{2}{3},$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n\nAll the other choices are sets.\n\n- $\\{\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\}$ is a set. The elements are $\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}$ and they are listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\{\\{\\}\\}$ is a set. It contains a single element, $\\{\\},$ and this element is between two braces.\n\n- $\\{\\}$ is a set. It is the null set.\n\n- $∅$ is a set. It is the null set." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514415 1068 17592186514226 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514415 1069 "q-344087" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514415 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514415 1071 "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each electron released in an atomic experiment. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular electron. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 \\\\ 0.832\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.832 \\\\ 0.555\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514415 1072 #uuid "68cd1b00-9920-5784-9707-6f682f05d636" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514415 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/17/171a9c86bf510c2ee5b65325d6f8939c65b5118286057934f91a053f75faf82f.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2} & 0.832{x}_{1}+0.555{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514416 1068 17592186514227 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514416 1069 "q-100238" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514416 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514416 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{({x}^{3}-6x+6)^{3}}{(2x+3)^{4}}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514416 1072 #uuid "6909c4f4-167d-501d-93f8-5aca9ebb11ed" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514416 1074 "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{({x}^{3}-6x+6)^{3}}{(2x+3)^{4}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -3}({x}^{3}-6x+6)^{3}}{{lim}_{x\\to -3}(2x+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -3}({x}^{3}-6x+6))}^{3}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -3}(2x+3))}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{((-3)^{3}-6(-3)+6)^{3}}{(2(-3)+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-27+18+6)^{3}}{(-6+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{(-3)^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514417 1068 17592186514235 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514417 1069 "q-2593" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514417 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514417 1071 "If $\\frac{8x-12y}{4}$ is written in the form $ax+by,$ what are the values of $a$ and $b?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514417 1072 #uuid "69e15305-fd39-5e12-9afd-c74d993158ed" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514417 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8x}{4}-\\frac{12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{4}\\cdot x-\\frac{12}{4}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2x-3y & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ax+by,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{a}x+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{b}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-3.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514418 1068 17592186514260 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514418 1069 "q-68919" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514418 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514418 1071 "At Joe's market, the unit price of tomatoes is $$5$ per pound. How many pounds of tomatoes can Geena buy for $$1?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514418 1072 #uuid "6ad6d7d2-b42a-533b-a893-138de3271ac7" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514418 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$5$ per pound (dollars per pound). To find how many pounds of tomatoes she can buy for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in pounds per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{dollars per pound}\\,\\to \\,1÷5=0.2\\,\\text{pounds per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.2$ pounds per dollar. Therefore, Geena can buy $0.2$ pounds of tomatoes for $1$ dollar." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514419 1068 17592186514276 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514419 1069 "q-101933" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514419 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514419 1071 "If ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},$ and ${2}^{9}$ are elements of $B$, but ${3}^{4}$ and ${5}^{0}$ are not, then which of the following is true?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514419 1072 #uuid "6ae10c4a-b478-5b9e-9599-7eb7b75dca9d" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514419 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},$ and ${2}^{9}$ are elements of $B,$ we write ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\in B.$\n\n- Since ${3}^{4}$ and ${5}^{0}$ are *not* elements of $B,$ we write ${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\notin B.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\in B$ and ${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\notin B$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514420 1068 17592186514289 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514420 1069 "q-101922" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514420 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514420 1071 "If $x,y,$ and $z$ are elements of $B$, but $v$ and $w$ are not, then which of the following is true?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514420 1072 #uuid "6b96bbb9-30d0-511e-a26c-5b394ffc1b3c" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514420 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $x,y,$ and $z$ are elements of $B,$ we write $x,y,z\\in B.$\n\n- Since $v$ and $w$ are *not* elements of $B,$ we write $v,w\\notin B.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $x,y,z\\in B$ and $v,w\\notin B$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514421 1068 17592186514341 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514421 1069 "q-14001" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514421 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514421 1071 "Find $p$ if $5$ is a root of $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+4px-10.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514421 1072 #uuid "6dc4cf16-d154-5217-ba51-01445eca1e9d" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514421 1074 "Since  $x=5$ is a root, we have $f(5)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=5,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(5)^{2}+4p(5)-10 & =0 \\\\ 6(25)+20p-10 & =0 \\\\ 150+20p-10 & =0 \\\\ 140+20p & =0 \\\\ 20p & =-140 \\\\ p & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514422 1068 17592186514310 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514422 1069 "q-166075" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514422 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514422 1071 "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=${{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514422 1072 #uuid "709bef3c-1deb-5177-94a0-6a0cd6bca192" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514422 1074 "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514423 1068 17592186514300 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514423 1069 "q-166079" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514423 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514423 1071 "$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514423 1072 #uuid "7323da16-6dcc-5329-81f1-5b6cb91bd332" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514423 1074 "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514424 1068 17592186514211 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514424 1069 "q-166074" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514424 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514424 1071 "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514424 1072 #uuid "745a29fd-db69-5896-9d5f-b9b3dcaa95b6" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514424 1074 "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 4, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514425 1068 17592186514279 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514425 1069 "q-104952" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514425 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514425 1071 "Which set represents all integers divisible by $5$ from $-21$ to $11?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514425 1072 #uuid "76066cd5-d606-50ce-8894-909f30c9f6a3" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514425 1074 "To express the desired set, we start with the first integer divisible by $5$ greater than or equal to $-21,$ which is $-20.$ The next integer divisible by $5$ is $-15,$ the one after that is $-10,$ and so on up to $10$ (which is the last number that is divisible by $5$ and less than or equal to $11$).\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{-20,-15,-10,…,10\\}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514426 1068 17592186514297 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514426 1069 "q-99475" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514426 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514426 1071 "$\\sqrt{81}=9$ can be expressed as" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514426 1072 #uuid "79a0a9c5-85f5-5fac-9044-aee801b38acc" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514426 1074 "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{81}^{1/2}=9\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${81}^{1/2}=9$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=9,b=81$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{81}=9$ is equivalent to ${log}_{81}⁡9=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514427 1068 17592186514257 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514427 1069 "q-112858" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514427 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514427 1071 "$(7+10\\,\\text{i})+(5+3\\,\\text{i})=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514427 1072 #uuid "7b28b38e-826f-526f-9fc1-5f33d99b85c5" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514427 1074 "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7+10\\,\\text{i})+(5+3\\,\\text{i}) & =(7+5)+(10+3)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =12+13\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514428 1068 17592186514321 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514428 1069 "q-113668" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514428 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514428 1071 "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{13}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514428 1072 #uuid "7c1117fb-96ed-5032-8c50-48772b44cf5c" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514428 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=13$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13+1}}{13+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{14}\\cdot 14\\,{x}^{13} & = \\\\ {x}^{13}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514429 1068 17592186514325 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514429 1069 "q-95682" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514429 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514429 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{3}}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514429 1072 #uuid "7c706f9d-3f1b-52c2-a133-ccf4032fcc0e" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514429 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{g},\\,g=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡s,\\,s={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}s}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g} & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}s}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the three results together and substituting for $g$ and $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡s\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{3}}{{sinh}^{2}⁡{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514430 1068 17592186514334 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514430 1069 "q-344079" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514430 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514430 1071 "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each oscillation produced when a mass hanging on a spring is displaced and then released. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular oscillation. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.456 \\\\ 0.890\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.890 \\\\ -0.456\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514430 1072 #uuid "7eb658cd-5383-5f28-8b76-9d708b2f6422" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514430 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f0/f0f0623703d1a3767a7c3efd275e977c6933465faeb761991f00da705bac4a2c.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2} & 0.890{x}_{1}-0.456{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514431 1068 17592186514232 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514431 1069 "q-66556" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514431 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514431 1071 "${3}^{0}=1$ can be expressed as" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514431 1072 #uuid "8064db11-eab8-5f4f-8e90-6d9982417bde" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514431 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${3}^{0}=1$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=1,b=3$ and $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${3}^{0}=1$ is equivalent to ${log}_{3}⁡1=0.$\n\nNote that ${log}_{n}⁡1=0$ for any $n>0,n\\ne 1.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514432 1068 17592186514326 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514432 1069 "q-148481" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514432 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514432 1071 "![](images/13/137e230421e20e7694526973fd4a92f9d573f1d97273151504df940b955d48f9.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514432 1072 #uuid "81392296-c859-50aa-9dad-b2d6c26af157" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514432 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $a$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5efefe99cb4fe0680d8ea3aaa31a05767f187e54acfb5b28145194e7dec24f3b.png)\n\n \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a$ and $c$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514433 1068 17592186514214 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514433 1069 "q-166071" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514433 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514433 1071 "$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=${{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514433 1072 #uuid "83dc5138-e470-5b8f-b97c-b3006ebeb428" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514433 1074 "We know that \n\n$$\n\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula and the constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514434 1068 17592186514209 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514434 1069 "q-335759" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514434 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514434 1071 "![](images/22/22acfe14709e4992ddadf6d1d4fcd0e2a533c93573c32dd4134ec3498b0ea805.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514434 1072 #uuid "87458c76-eabf-5005-ac30-a442db3ab7b3" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514434 1074 "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $-3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $-3.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514435 1068 17592186514284 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514435 1069 "q-78875" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514435 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514435 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x=22?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514435 1072 #uuid "8b1e4f44-4956-589a-9c17-f83b0ba4a3a8" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514435 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x & =22 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+12x & =22 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2[{x}^{2}-6x] & =22 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2[(x-3)^{2}-9] & =22 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2(x-3)^{2}+18 & =22 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2(x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(3,0).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514436 1068 17592186514222 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514436 1069 "q-95674" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514436 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514436 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514436 1072 #uuid "925db400-88a9-5795-a4b0-aad7734a8104" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514436 1074 "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514437 1068 17592186514282 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514437 1069 "q-96551" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514437 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514437 1071 "$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to  $\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2w)?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514437 1072 #uuid "9460a409-51fa-5175-bdf4-3dbd6c50a02e" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514437 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+1}{{e}^{2x}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2x}}{1-{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the third definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2w) & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2w}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2w}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4{w}^{2}}+1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4{w}^{2}}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{w}^{2}+1}{4{w}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514438 1068 17592186514219 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514438 1069 "q-149395" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514438 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514438 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $17$ and $7.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514438 1072 #uuid "95e787d7-1400-508c-9805-e2c36a0d2bcb" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514438 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=17$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514439 1068 17592186514293 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514439 1069 "q-142729" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514439 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514439 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{25},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.01.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514439 1072 #uuid "9663f7f5-26ed-525b-a4e7-8ebcf7b4896a" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514439 1074 "![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.01$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.01\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.005.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{25},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=25.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $1.318$ | $1.711$ | $2.064$ | $2.492$ | $2.797$ |\n| $25$ | $1.316$ | $1.708$ | $2.060$ | $2.485$ | $2.787$ |\n| $26$ | $1.315$ | $1.706$ | $2.056$ | $2.479$ | $2.779$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.787)=0.005.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.787.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514440 1068 17592186514220 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514440 1069 "q-105944" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514440 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514440 1071 "![](images/7d/7defdf7c31a164fdbe702c1f5e87567f5da868d7530143493f9713ff88de98bc.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)$ for the function above." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514440 1072 #uuid "9722b50e-b540-58f7-8d1d-e4a47908bdb3" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514440 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(-\\frac{3}{2})$ is $0.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $2.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514441 1068 17592186514347 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514441 1069 "q-335712" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514441 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514441 1071 "![](images/c3/c336dbdbda8ea15f5d2e86eb75493690d11ad0080c3ceeac9f71ab48cb63737b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514441 1072 #uuid "9c2e4638-f161-548b-867c-b6be12db26ee" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514441 1074 "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514442 1068 17592186514336 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514442 1069 "q-109858" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514442 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514442 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ for all compatible matrices $A$ and $B?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A+B)$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A+B)$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514442 1072 #uuid "9d632f6e-3556-5d97-834c-4e1de9c56681" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514442 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ is the row space of $({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})^{T}=A+B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $A+B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $A+B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $A+B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $A+B,$ we actually multiply each row of $A+B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $A+B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(A+B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514443 1068 17592186514271 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514443 1069 "q-109748" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514443 1070 17592186045486 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514443 1071 "The weights of some chocolate cookies are normally distributed with a mean of $10\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $1\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weights of some oatcakes are normally distributed with a mean of $15\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $2\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$  If one chocolate cookie and one oatcake are randomly selected, what is the probability that $6$ times the weight of the cookie plus $4$ times the weight of oatcake is less than $120\\,\\text{g}$, but more than $100\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of the cookies and oatcakes are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0062\\,$ | $\\,0.0060\\,$ | $\\,0.0059\\,$ | $\\,0.0057\\,$ | $\\,0.0055\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0082\\,$ | $\\,0.0080\\,$ | $\\,0.0078\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0107\\,$ | $\\,0.0104\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514443 1072 #uuid "9dd9198d-6665-5e5e-bf17-48bb8a7b15be" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514443 1074 "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chocolate cookie, and let $Y$ denote the weight of an oatcake. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(100<6X+4Y<120).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,1)$ and $Y∼N(15,2),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6X+4Y & ∼N(6\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 15,36\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(120,68).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $6X+4Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =120$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{68}.$\n\nTo find $P(100<6X+4Y<120),$ we convert $6X+4Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & =P(\\frac{100-120}{\\sqrt{68}}<Z<\\frac{120-120}{\\sqrt{68}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.43<Z<0) \\\\  & =P(Z<0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.43) & ≈0.0075,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & ≈Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43) \\\\  & =0.5-0.0075 \\\\  & ≈0.4925.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1068 17592186514210 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1068 17592186514215 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1068 17592186514228 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1068 17592186514251 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1068 17592186514283 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1068 17592186514348 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1068 17592186514349 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1069 "q-335018" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-0.5x+0.5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.2,\\,1]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is\n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1072 #uuid "9ec6a2eb-63e4-55a4-849b-ba0f89289e7d" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514444 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-0.5x+0.5xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.5x+0.5xy)=-0.5+0.5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.5x+0.5xy)=0.5x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-5xy)=1-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5y & 0.5x \\\\ -5y & 1-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5(1) & 0.5(0.2) \\\\ -5(1) & 1-5(0.2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-5)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514445 1068 17592186514267 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514445 1069 "q-148519" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514445 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514445 1071 "![](images/a8/a84a2fa07d06dce3026740be2831127fd4d7f2d72fd31388df646ad7ac62e4ff.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514445 1072 #uuid "a40aacc4-bbfa-50e9-8365-3467adc8fc1b" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514445 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $a$ or to the vector $c,$ as shown below. So, $a$ or $c$ define the direction of the first principal component. \n\n![](images/4d/4d025f8b6447b7a17e4c5fcfb192dcaf4c2bb1ea3a926961d25e94832972bee1.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8d223a9a8a29f297a88f7d7c98d99776540e0ba0e0f3935892a356e92a8aac9.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514446 1068 17592186514340 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514446 1069 "q-29994" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514446 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514446 1071 "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{4}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514446 1072 #uuid "a4e1eabf-8b25-5ba2-8660-9b7cb6a5610c" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514446 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514447 1068 17592186514306 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514447 1069 "q-165769" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514447 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514447 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{4}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514447 1072 #uuid "a7015904-a594-5558-b15e-195c0d212c67" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514447 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{4}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{3x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{4}+{e}^{3x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{4}+{e}^{3x}\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{4} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡{x}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514448 1068 17592186514273 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514448 1069 "q-300677" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514448 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514448 1071 "$$\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n$\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514448 1072 #uuid "a717dc62-f0d0-579e-9ded-2923a5f96442" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514448 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{5})^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}+(\\sqrt{5})^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514449 1068 17592186514242 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514449 1069 "q-300702" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514449 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514449 1071 "Evaluate $\\operatorname{coth}⁡3.$ **Do not round** your answer.\n$$$$\n\n$\\operatorname{coth}⁡3=$\n {{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514449 1072 #uuid "a8d94db5-30b8-5d58-91bd-e8bbc8dd17c5" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514449 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+1}{{e}^{2x}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2x}}{1-{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the third definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡3 & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3)}+1}{{e}^{2(3)}-1}=\\frac{{e}^{6}+1}{{e}^{6}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514450 1068 17592186514291 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514450 1069 "q-250190" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514450 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514450 1071 "Evaluate the following expression. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$(6-4\\,\\text{i})-3=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514450 1072 #uuid "a8e5bfd0-3749-5d98-b80f-2065e3b9d3d1" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514450 1074 "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6-4\\,\\text{i})-3 & =(6-4\\,\\text{i})+(-3+0\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(6-3)+(-4+0)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =3+(-4)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =3-4\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514451 1068 17592186514252 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514451 1069 "q-340820" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514451 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514451 1071 "$$\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{sech}⁡1.$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n$\\operatorname{sech}⁡1=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514451 1072 #uuid "aa7fb149-1626-56db-aafd-3693bf4f703f" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514451 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡1 & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{1}+{e}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(e+\\frac{1}{e})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{e})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{{e}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514452 1068 17592186514314 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514452 1069 "q-149394" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514452 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514452 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $3$ and $5.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514452 1072 #uuid "b0942e9b-0d31-57a8-9f11-cdccb8f6633f" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514452 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514453 1068 17592186514253 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514453 1069 "q-91913" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514453 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514453 1071 "In a snack store, there are three different types of cookies that come in boxes: \n\n- Box $A$ contains $13$ pounds, and costs $$3.77\\,.$\n\n- Box $B$ contains $10.5$ pounds, and costs $$3.15\\,.$\n\n- Box $C$ contains $12$ pounds, and costs $$3.48\\,.$\n\nWhich box of cookies is the best deal?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514453 1072 #uuid "b32ba521-b62e-5f22-bb06-26d27c73470d" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514453 1074 "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three boxes. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of pounds.  \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in box $A$,  we get\n\n$$\n3.77÷13=0.29\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $A$ is $$0.29$  per pound.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in box $B$,  we get\n\n$$\n3.15÷10.5=0.30\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $B$ is $$0.30$  per pound.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in box $C$,  we get\n\n$$\n3.48÷12=0.29\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $C$ is $$0.29$  per pound.\n\nFrom the above, we see that boxes $A$ and $C$ have the lowest unit price. Therefore, boxes $A$ and $C$ are the best deals." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514454 1068 17592186514346 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514454 1069 "q-148488" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514454 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514454 1071 "![](images/dc/dc03c3e8b7769622b363924604f242d0cf7f80eab6e0019eba5a3e95d6bc3c10.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514454 1072 #uuid "b3de56e3-5772-5207-831f-4a8a7dca824e" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514454 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $a,$ as shown below. So, $a$ defines the direction of the first principal component. \n\n![](images/4f/4f751057ec2ba51c7132b75ed4e0309269288601ebf19b4f13de0a5b54e6c615.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c272bdfda7665b4892155335ead5cf79b08b98e19a04a6211f2a628a0161c28.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514455 1068 17592186514304 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514455 1069 "q-2596" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514455 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514455 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{4a-6b}{4}?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514455 1072 #uuid "b8beba6a-7b91-5602-93f9-62eba4e754b8" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514455 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4a-6b}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4}-\\frac{6b}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}\\cdot a-\\frac{6}{4}\\cdot b & = \\\\ a-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot b & = \\\\ a-\\frac{3b}{2} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514456 1068 17592186514354 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514456 1069 "q-66572" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514456 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514456 1071 "${3}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{27}$ can be expressed as" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514456 1072 #uuid "bb68a7c2-cb48-5196-952e-fea6e54d1914" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514456 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${3}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{27}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{27},b=3$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{27})=-3.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514457 1068 17592186514316 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514457 1069 "q-618" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514457 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514457 1071 "$(8-\\sqrt{-4})-(-7+\\sqrt{-49})=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514457 1072 #uuid "bbd97f04-776a-53ed-8d9f-1d2d316080bc" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514457 1074 "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-\\sqrt{-4})-(-7+\\sqrt{-49}) & =(8-2\\,\\text{i})-(-7+7\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(8-(-7))+(-2-7)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =(8+7)-9\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =15-9\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514458 1068 17592186514212 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514458 1069 "q-125698" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514458 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514458 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $t>0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t})?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514458 1072 #uuid "bc92dd53-1396-50af-a160-bf5a5b938587" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514458 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{t})^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}+(\\sqrt{t})^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{t}}{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514459 1068 17592186514320 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514459 1069 "q-66504" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514459 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514459 1071 "$\\sqrt{2}≈1.414$ can be expressed as" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514459 1072 #uuid "be53ea1d-fce1-50b2-b27f-f485b9e423e6" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514459 1074 "Notice that the given approximation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{2}^{1/2}≈1.412\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${2}^{1/2}≈1.412$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=1.412,b=2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the approximation $\\sqrt{2}≈1.414$ can be expressed as ${log}_{2}⁡(1.414)≈\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514460 1068 17592186514280 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514460 1069 "q-181262" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514460 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514460 1071 "Which of the following is an element of the set $A=\\{♢,\\,10,\\,a,\\,◻\\}?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514460 1072 #uuid "bf7a3bf9-7841-5c39-8953-649f06640b22" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514460 1074 "The elements of our set are as follows:\n\n$$\n♢,\\,10,\\,a,\\,◻\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, the only element that belongs to the set is $◻.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514461 1068 17592186514302 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514461 1069 "q-175410" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514461 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514461 1071 "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-3,1)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(1,2)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}?$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514461 1072 #uuid "c53663ac-ef5b-58bc-a1be-8cea3868fa2f" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514461 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 1,{1}^{2}\\cdot 1+{3}^{2}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(0,19).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(0,19).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514462 1068 17592186514274 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514462 1069 "q-68863" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514462 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514462 1071 "Jack bought a pumpkin that weighed $3$ kilograms. The retail price of pumpkin at the store is  $$1.78/kg.$ How much did  Jack pay for the pumpkin?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514462 1072 #uuid "c993b31b-1739-52ac-a2f5-55deea55eaf7" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514462 1074 "The unit price of pumpkin is $$1.78/kg.$ To find the cost of  $3$ kilograms of pumpkin, we multiply:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.78\\times 3 & =5.34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jack paid  $$5.34$ for the $3$-$\\text{kg}$ pumpkin." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514463 1068 17592186514305 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514463 1069 "q-71405" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514463 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514463 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514463 1072 #uuid "cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514463 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,k \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz))\\text{ }j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(-(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0))\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514464 1068 17592186514345 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514464 1069 "q-166073" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514464 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514464 1071 "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514464 1072 #uuid "d1eaed8d-b238-536c-b3a6-a6e7f9e94a12" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514464 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514465 1068 17592186514241 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514465 1069 "q-142541" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514465 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514465 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{19},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.9.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514465 1072 #uuid "d5be6574-3a28-52d0-8e4b-f267e2db636b" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514465 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X\\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.9 \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{19},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=19.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 1.328)=0.100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.328.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514466 1068 17592186514216 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514466 1068 17592186514331 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514466 1069 "q-166077" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514466 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514466 1071 "![](images/5d/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ when $\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514466 1072 #uuid "d78b3b66-caba-5b75-9179-7631fdc0e796" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514466 1074 "A stationary point of $g(x)$ is a value of $x$ where ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/36/36fd58470a5361250fa7852d92abe2b9c2dbf18ca97d00e51ea4013f8f3aa348.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates (in ascending order) are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514467 1068 17592186514295 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514467 1069 "q-344073" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514467 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514467 1071 "A meteorologist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ at various locations throughout California. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular location. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.745\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.745 \\\\ 0.667\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the meteorologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514467 1072 #uuid "dd2cd7be-06ce-5ffb-8860-cd5d1eb5c130" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514467 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c823606bbbe77326819df5d487b5e0019dd7439b46124547f71681b9484f6d.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2} & -0.745{x}_{1}+0.667{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514468 1068 17592186514329 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514468 1069 "q-43064" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514468 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514468 1071 "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514468 1072 #uuid "e03bdc50-8826-55ed-961a-7b04fba13b73" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514468 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =20+2 \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{22}.\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514469 1068 17592186514324 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514469 1069 "q-34927" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514469 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514469 1071 "![](images/67/6769c6590446a9599786758ba9cfaf17690f401d864b3ce5f2c3825d9ca432cd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514469 1072 #uuid "e220bff6-4f48-5639-8c40-6889828b8e5d" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514469 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514470 1068 17592186514318 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514470 1069 "q-130697" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514470 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514470 1071 "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+5 & 5 \\\\ b & -3\\end{aligned}|=-34$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+4 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}|.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514470 1072 #uuid "e3cff1af-b64e-5d5d-98b9-281348f5cad1" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514470 1074 "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+4 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+5)-1 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+5)-1 & 5 \\\\ b+2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a+5 & 5 \\\\ b & -3\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-34}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-34+(3-10) \\\\  & =-41.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514471 1068 17592186514311 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514471 1069 "q-95326" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514471 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514471 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $t(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514471 1072 #uuid "e582897a-43fa-53c9-83f2-0eb49fceb2e4" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514471 1074 "In this case, the expression for $t(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{t}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot ({e}^{x})^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514472 1068 17592186514261 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514472 1069 "q-138505" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514472 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514472 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $4$ and $2.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514472 1072 #uuid "e7322fc5-138d-596a-9e83-0b51cc3f5715" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514472 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514473 1068 17592186514308 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514473 1069 "q-597" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514473 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514473 1071 "$(9+8\\,\\text{i})-(6+4\\,\\text{i})=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514473 1072 #uuid "ef858a39-0a28-52af-9941-ce2f2a8713ef" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514473 1074 "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9+8\\,\\text{i})-(6+4\\,\\text{i}) & =(9-6)+(8-4)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =3+4\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514474 1068 17592186514263 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514474 1069 "q-80286" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514474 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514474 1071 "If $\\pi ,\\text{e}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are elements of $Y$, but $0$ and $1$ are not, then which of the following is true?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514474 1072 #uuid "f10fdf14-c3b0-5ff7-a1e1-67a02a7a3852" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514474 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $\\pi ,\\text{e}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are elements of $Y,$ we write $\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\in Y.$\n\n- Since $0$ and $1$ are *not* elements of $Y,$ we write $0,1\\notin Y.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\in Y$ and $0,1\\notin Y$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514475 1068 17592186514290 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514475 1069 "q-80275" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514475 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514475 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a set?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514475 1072 #uuid "f2f32264-6492-5e60-8c09-298d7626357d" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514475 1074 "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $∅.$\n\n- $f,g,h$ is *not* a set. The letters $f,$ $g,$ and $h$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n\nAll the other choices are sets.\n\n- $\\{f,\\{h\\}\\}$ is a set. The elements are $f,$ and $\\{h\\},$ and they are listed between the two outermost braces.\n\n- $\\{∅,\\{f,g\\}\\}$ is a set. The elements are $∅,$ and $\\{f,g\\},$ and are listed between the two outermost braces.\n\n- $\\{\\}$ is a set. It is the null set.\n\n- $\\{1,f,2,g\\}$ is a set. The elements are $1,$ $f,$ $2,$ and $g,$ and they are listed between two braces." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514476 1068 17592186514234 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514476 1069 "q-108048" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514476 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514476 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{16})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ can be expressed as" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514476 1072 #uuid "f3941239-6e19-5b0b-909a-0a8f6dea7adb" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514476 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${(\\frac{1}{16})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{16},b=\\frac{1}{16}$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${(\\frac{1}{16})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{1/16}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=1.$\n\nNote that ${log}_{n}⁡n=1$ for any $n>0,n\\ne 1.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514477 1068 17592186514268 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514477 1069 "q-95683" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514477 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514477 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514477 1072 #uuid "f4f3ca36-fa30-5fa4-b1a8-a0cc296aaddc" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514477 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{g},\\,g=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=-\\frac{2}{{g}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2}{{g}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514478 1068 17592186514213 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514478 1069 "q-95321" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514478 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514478 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $t(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{x}.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514478 1072 #uuid "f50f96cb-b22b-5bd9-91c4-b27ea7e844e6" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514478 1074 "In this case, the expression for $t(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ and $v(x)=x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{t}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot (x)^{′}}{(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514479 1068 17592186514258 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514479 1069 "q-300752" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514479 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514479 1071 "![](images/93/93fc83d79679ca9f2b58729af1e3e87c2a01c19c12bc97670fbff398d63d061d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514479 1072 #uuid "f5f74f57-aaaa-5a36-a7e2-f4e15eb6a61c" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514479 1074 "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $0.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514480 1068 17592186514322 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514480 1069 "q-340828" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514480 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514480 1071 "$$\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{coth}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n$\\operatorname{coth}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2})=$\n {{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514480 1072 #uuid "f714102a-964b-532c-b041-8149ac6f2160" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514480 1074 "$$\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+1}{{e}^{2x}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2x}}{1-{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fourth definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2})}}{1-{e}^{-2(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}}}{1-{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}}{1-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{1}{4})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514481 1068 17592186514266 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514481 1069 "q-68901" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514481 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514481 1071 "Lucy's Treats sells three different boxes of raisins: \n\n- Box $A$ contains $12$ ounces, and costs $$2.28\\,.$\n\n- Box $B$ contains $32$ ounces,  and costs $$5.44\\,.$\n\n- Box $C$ contains $22$ ounces, and costs $$3.52\\,.$\n\nWhich box of raisins is the best deal?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514481 1072 #uuid "fa60ae23-8081-566e-ab07-1797ceb80d43" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514481 1074 "The best deal is the box with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three boxes. To compute the unit price, we divide the price by the total number of ounces.   \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n2.28÷12=0.19\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $A$ is $$0.19.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n5.44÷32=0.17\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $B$ is $$0.17.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.52÷22=0.16\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $C$ is $$0.16.$\n\nFrom the above, we see that box $C$ has the lowest unit price of $$0.16/\\text{oz}.$ Therefore, box $C$ offers the best deal." 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514482 1068 17592186514248 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514482 1069 "q-125696" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514482 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514482 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514482 1072 #uuid "fab073b9-61b0-51f3-a69e-3645145ce1fa" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514482 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}}-{e}^{-(-\\sqrt{3})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}}-{e}^{\\sqrt{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514483 1068 17592186514256 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514483 1069 "q-166070" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514483 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514483 1071 "The function $p(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ g(x), & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind the value of the constant $k$ that makes $p(x)$ continuous at $x=3.$\n\n$k\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514483 1072 #uuid "fbda5c07-3c3f-5393-8bd3-32f9b514a1ca" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514483 1074 "We have the piecewise function\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ 8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & =p(3) \\\\  & =k+10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(3)-\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3} \\\\  & =24-18 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $p(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) \\\\ k+10 & =6 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ when $k=-4.$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514484 1068 17592186514353 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514484 1069 "q-138131" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514484 1070 17592186045485 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514484 1071 "The maximum  value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum  is attained?" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514484 1072 #uuid "fd31a01e-94fe-56c0-b345-2a845f22f0d5" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514484 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514485 1068 17592186514262 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514485 1069 "q-340819" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514485 1070 17592186045487 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514485 1071 "$$\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n$\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514485 1072 #uuid "ff24eb5c-4023-53e8-863a-4c063695bc62" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514485 1074 "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}-{e}^{-(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\sqrt{5})}^{-1}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1-5}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =2\\times (-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514486 1058 17592186514522 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514486 1062 #uuid "0526a648-ee54-5a70-b790-abd5fc388aa7" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514486 1063 17592186162271 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514487 1058 17592186514534 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514487 1062 #uuid "06f1059b-36c3-52dd-8a44-9cfe68b4b236" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514487 1063 17592186514468 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514488 1058 17592186514539 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514488 1062 #uuid "09e477d4-799c-59d3-9b5b-d55710b82507" 13194139535541 17592186045417 true] [17592186514488 1063 17592186514412 1